CAT = "uk3fsLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
Q = [
("An officer serves a hygiene improvement notice. What is the shortest time it can allow to put things right?",
 "14 days", ["7 days", "21 days", "28 days"],
 "A hygiene improvement notice must give at least 14 days to carry out the measures it lists. Failing to comply after that is an offence."),
("What does a hygiene improvement notice have to set out for the food business?",
 "The breach and the fix needed", ["The fine the court will impose", "The new hygiene rating score", "A date for a court hearing"],
 "The notice states the officer's grounds, the matters that break the law, the measures needed and the time allowed. It does not set a fine or a rating."),
("A business ignores a hygiene improvement notice past its deadline. What is the result?",
 "It commits an offence", ["The notice is extended once", "Its rating drops to zero", "The officer must warn it first"],
 "Failing to comply with a hygiene improvement notice is an offence in itself, so the business can be prosecuted."),
("Which notice lets an officer close food premises at once, before any court hearing?",
 "Emergency prohibition notice", ["Hygiene improvement notice", "Hygiene prohibition order", "Remedial action notice to stop work"],
 "An emergency prohibition notice takes effect when served if there is an imminent risk to health. A hygiene prohibition order follows a conviction in court."),
("An emergency prohibition notice lapses if the officer has not applied to court within:",
 "3 days", ["24 hours", "7 days", "14 days"],
 "If no application for an emergency prohibition order is made within three days of service, the notice lapses."),
("Who makes a hygiene emergency prohibition order?",
 "A magistrates' court", ["The environmental health officer", "The Food Standards Agency", "The council's licensing team"],
 "The officer serves the notice, but only a magistrates' court can make the emergency prohibition order."),
("A closed business has removed the health risk. What lets it reopen?",
 "A certificate from the authority", ["A new hygiene rating", "A letter from its own pest contractor", "An apology to the court"],
 "An emergency prohibition notice or order ends when the enforcement authority issues a certificate that enough has been done to remove the risk."),
("After a court conviction, which order can ban a food business operator from running a food business?",
 "Hygiene prohibition order", ["Hygiene improvement notice", "Emergency prohibition notice", "Detention notice"],
 "A court that convicts can make a hygiene prohibition order, which may stop a process, close premises or bar the operator from managing a food business."),
("Under the food hygiene rating scheme, what does a rating of 3 mean?",
 "Generally satisfactory", ["Very good", "Some improvement necessary", "Major improvement necessary"],
 "The scale runs from 5, very good, to 0, urgent improvement required. A 3 means hygiene standards are generally satisfactory."),
("An officer believes a batch of cooked meat is unsafe. What power lets them take it away?",
 "Seizing it for a magistrate", ["Ordering a deep clean of the area", "Serving an improvement notice", "Lowering the hygiene rating"],
 "Under the Food Safety Act 1990 an officer can detain or seize suspect food, and a justice of the peace can order it destroyed."),
("To rely on due diligence, a business must show it took all reasonable precautions and:",
 "Exercised all due diligence", ["Had no earlier customer complaints", "Held a 5 rating at the time", "Paid for an external audit"],
 "The defence has two parts: all reasonable precautions were taken and all due diligence was exercised to avoid the offence."),
("Which evidence best supports a due diligence defence?",
 "Signed monitoring records", ["A recent hygiene rating sticker", "Staff saying the checks were done", "A cleaning contract on file"],
 "Courts look for written proof that controls were in place and checked. Signed records made at the time are far stronger than verbal claims."),
("An offence happened because a supplier delivered contaminated food. Which defence might apply?",
 "The fault was another person's", ["The food was given free of charge", "The customer did not complain", "The food was only a sample"],
 "The law lets a business argue the offence was due to someone else's act or default, but it must still show it took reasonable precautions itself."),
("During an inspection, what should a supervisor do?",
 "Go round with them, taking notes", ["Leave the officer to work alone", "Ask the officer to come back later", "Clean each area ahead of the officer"],
 "Going round with the officer lets you answer questions, find records and note every point raised so action can follow."),
("Which three areas decide a food hygiene rating in England, Wales and Northern Ireland?",
 "Handling, premises and management", ["Prices, menu range and service", "Staff numbers, sales and complaints", "Size, age and location"],
 "The officer scores how food is handled, the physical condition of the premises and how food safety is managed and recorded."),
("A business has improved since a poor rating. What may it ask the authority for?",
 "A revisit to re-rate it", ["The old rating to be deleted", "A free training course", "A 5 rating for the next year"],
 "After making the required improvements, a business can request a re-rating visit. It can also appeal a rating it thinks is unfair."),
("A business accepts its rating but wants to explain the reasons. What can it use?",
 "Its right to reply", ["A formal appeal", "A complaint to the FSA", "An improvement notice"],
 "The right to reply lets a business publish its comments next to its rating online, for example about work done since the visit."),
("A customer says your food made them ill. What should the supervisor do first?",
 "Record details, keep suspect food", ["Offer a refund and close the matter", "Bin the dish to stop any more cases", "Admit fault and apologise in writing"],
 "Write down what was eaten, when, the symptoms and contact details, and keep any remaining suspect food chilled and labelled for the EHO."),
("In a food poisoning outbreak, what is the EHO's main aim?",
 "To stop the illness spreading", ["To decide the compensation owed", "To change the hygiene rating", "To find someone to prosecute"],
 "The investigation looks for the food, the cause and the source so the outbreak can be stopped. Enforcement may follow, but control comes first."),
("A customer complains of a hair in a dish. Which record helps most later?",
 "A written complaint log", ["The menu for that day", "The staff rota for the week", "The cleaning chemical list"],
 "A complaint log with the date, food, item found, action taken and outcome shows how complaints are handled and helps spot repeats."),
("Which offence covers selling food that is not what the customer asked for?",
 "Not of the nature demanded", ["Rendering food injurious to health", "Falsely presenting the food", "Selling food unfit to eat"],
 "Serving cod when haddock was ordered is food not of the nature demanded, under the Food Safety Act 1990."),
("A menu calls frozen fish fresh. Which offence is this most likely to be?",
 "Falsely describing food", ["Not of the substance demanded", "Rendering food injurious", "Obstructing an officer"],
 "Describing or presenting food in a way that misleads about its nature or quality is an offence under the Food Safety Act 1990."),
("Under retained Regulation 852/2004, what must most food businesses put in place?",
 "Procedures based on HACCP", ["A licence from the FSA", "A Level 3 qualified manager", "An annual external audit"],
 "Article 5 requires permanent procedures based on the HACCP principles. The law asks for suitable training, not a set qualification."),
("Which law turns food hygiene breaches into offences in England?",
 "Food Safety and Hygiene Regs 2013", ["Health and Safety at Work Act 1974", "Consumer Rights Act 2015", "Food Information Regulations 2014"],
 "The Food Safety and Hygiene (England) Regulations 2013 enforce the retained EU hygiene rules and set the notices, offences and penalties."),
("Under the general food law, what must a business be able to show about its suppliers?",
 "Who supplied each food to it", ["The supplier's hygiene rating", "The supplier's profit margins", "Where its staff trained"],
 "Traceability means a business must be able to identify who supplied its food and which businesses it supplied, one step back and one step forward."),
("Who must have adequate training in applying the HACCP principles?",
 "Those who run the procedures", ["Every member of front-of-house staff", "Only the registered owner", "Only the outside auditor"],
 "Hygiene law requires those responsible for developing and maintaining the HACCP-based procedures to be adequately trained in the principles."),
("Unsafe food from one batch has gone to other businesses. What must the operator do?",
 "Withdraw it and tell the authority", ["Wait for an officer to ask about it", "Relabel it and keep selling it", "Tell only the head office"],
 "Under general food law, a business that thinks food it supplied is unsafe must start a withdrawal or recall and inform the competent authority."),
("A caller asks a business in Wales for its hygiene rating. What must it do?",
 "Tell the caller its rating", ["Refer them to the FSA website", "Refuse until the next visit", "Post them the sticker"],
 "In Wales and Northern Ireland a business must display its rating and also give it verbally when asked in person or by phone."),
]
